  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unsettled weather continues into the week.\n\nDiurnal thunderstorm activity to continue into this week as warm and \nmoist conditions remain the area. A mid-level low over Tennessee \nwill send some vorticity to the region this afternoon, though \nthe spatial coverage of rain looks to be mostly confined in the \nmountains of northwest North Carolina and far southwestern \nVirginia. This area may also have some isolated wet microbursts \nwithin the storms and the Storm Prediction Center has issued a \nMarginal Risk of Severe Weather due to this hazard. Because \nPWATs continue to be around 1.50-2.00\" in North Carolina and the\nPiedmont, there still remains an excessive rainfall risk for \nthis part of the Mid-Atlantic for today.\n\nMonday was the most frustrating part of the forecast as CAMs had \ndisagreement on the precipitation chances. Much of the discrepancy \nwas due to uncertainty on how long the vorticity from the low to our \nsouthwest would reside in VA/NC and how early an approaching line of \nthunderstorms would approach from the northwest. Precipitation \nchances remain low due to this uncertainty, but these values may \nshoot up if future model guidance come into better agreement.\n\nRegardless on how today and Monday play out, what is clear is a \nridge weakening over the southwestern CONUS and another ridge over \nthe southeast/Bermuda moving to the south. With a mid-level \ncutoff low over Canada to our north, the troughs and shortwaves \nthat orbit it will likely migrate just southward to intercept \nour area. The arrival of several waves for the latter half of \nthe week means more additional chances of thunderstorms, some \nwhich may be severe thanks to some addition of wind shear. Of \nparticular note is a few low level jets may also accompany these\nwaves and bring wind gusts of 25-40 mph along and west of the \nBlue Ridge. Specifics on hazards regarding winds, severe, and \nflooding will be narrowed down as we head into the week.",
